### Why not retry in place on the main consumer?

Blocking retries on the main consumer stall the whole partition while sleeping between attempts. One slow record delays everything behind it. Retry topics solve this by moving the waiting off the main consumer.

```java
package com.bank.retry;

public class BlockingVsNonBlocking {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Blocking retry (BAD):");
        System.out.println("  offset 100: process -> FAIL");
        System.out.println("  sleep 1s, retry -> FAIL");
        System.out.println("  sleep 5s, retry -> FAIL");
        System.out.println("  Partition STALLED for 6+ seconds");
        System.out.println("  offset 101, 102, 103 waiting...");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Non-blocking retry topics (GOOD):");
        System.out.println("  offset 100: process -> FAIL");
        System.out.println("  Publish to retry.1s, commit past 100");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: process -> OK (immediately!)");
        System.out.println("  offset 102: process -> OK");
        System.out.println("  Retry consumer picks up offset 100 after 1s");
    }
}
```

```text
Blocking retry (BAD):
  offset 100: process -> FAIL
  sleep 1s, retry -> FAIL
  sleep 5s, retry -> FAIL
  Partition STALLED for 6+ seconds
  offset 101, 102, 103 waiting...

Non-blocking retry topics (GOOD):
  offset 100: process -> FAIL
  Publish to retry.1s, commit past 100
  offset 101: process -> OK (immediately!)
  offset 102: process -> OK
  Retry consumer picks up offset 100 after 1s
```

### How does the retry ladder work?

Each tier has a longer delay. A record carries a header counting attempts. Each failure increments the count and graduates the record to the next tier. When the last tier is exhausted, it goes to the DLQ.

```java
package com.bank.retry;

public class RetryLadder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Tier layout for transfers topic:");
        System.out.println("  transfers          (main, no delay)");
        System.out.println("  transfers-retry-1s (1 second delay)");
        System.out.println("  transfers-retry-5s (5 second delay)");
        System.out.println("  transfers-retry-30s (30 second delay)");
        System.out.println("  transfers.DLT      (dead letter queue)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Flow for a transient failure:");
        System.out.println("  main: FAIL -> publish to retry-1s, commit");
        System.out.println("  retry-1s: FAIL -> publish to retry-5s");
        System.out.println("  retry-5s: FAIL -> publish to retry-30s");
        System.out.println("  retry-30s: FAIL -> publish to DLT");
    }
}
```

```text
Tier layout for transfers topic:
  transfers          (main, no delay)
  transfers-retry-1s (1 second delay)
  transfers-retry-5s (5 second delay)
  transfers-retry-30s (30 second delay)
  transfers.DLT      (dead letter queue)

Flow for a transient failure:
  main: FAIL -> publish to retry-1s, commit
  retry-1s: FAIL -> publish to retry-5s
  retry-5s: FAIL -> publish to retry-30s
  retry-30s: FAIL -> publish to DLT
```

### Why use exponential backoff?

Exponential backoff gives a failing dependency time to recover. Short waits first, longer waits later, so you do not hammer a struggling service.

```java
package com.bank.retry;

public class ExponentialBackoff {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] delays = {1000, 5000, 30000};
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < delays.length; i++) {
            total += delays[i];
            System.out.printf("  Attempt %d: wait %d ms (total elapsed: %d ms)%n",
                i + 1, delays[i], total);
        }
        System.out.println("  Total retry window: " + total / 1000 + " seconds");
        System.out.println("  Dependency has 36 seconds to recover");
    }
}
```

```text
  Attempt 1: wait 1000 ms (total elapsed: 1000 ms)
  Attempt 2: wait 5000 ms (total elapsed: 6000 ms)
  Attempt 3: wait 30000 ms (total elapsed: 36000 ms)
  Total retry window: 36 seconds
  Dependency has 36 seconds to recover
```

### Why add jitter?

Without jitter, if many records fail at the same moment because a dependency went down, they all retry at the same instant when their identical delays elapse. That thundering herd can knock the dependency over again.

```java
package com.bank.retry;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class JitterDemo {
    public static long withJitter(long baseDelay) {
        long jitter = (long) (baseDelay * 0.5);
        return baseDelay - jitter + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(2 * jitter);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long baseDelay = 5000;
        System.out.println("Base delay: " + baseDelay + " ms");
        System.out.println("With jitter (5 samples):");
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("  Retry " + (i + 1) + ": " + withJitter(baseDelay) + " ms");
        }
        System.out.println("Retries are SPREAD -- no thundering herd");
    }
}
```

```text
Base delay: 5000 ms
With jitter (5 samples):
  Retry 1: 3821 ms
  Retry 2: 6244 ms
  Retry 3: 4517 ms
  Retry 4: 7012 ms
  Retry 5: 2958 ms
Retries are SPREAD -- no thundering herd
```

### How do you classify failures?

```java
package com.bank.retry;

public class FailureClassification {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Transient (enter retry ladder):");
        System.out.println("  - java.net.SocketTimeoutException");
        System.out.println("  - org.springframework.dao.QueryTimeoutException");
        System.out.println("  - HTTP 503 Service Unavailable");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Permanent (straight to DLQ):");
        System.out.println("  - IllegalArgumentException (validation)");
        System.out.println("  - SerializationException (bad format)");
        System.out.println("  - HTTP 400 Bad Request");
    }
}
```

```text
Transient (enter retry ladder):
  - java.net.SocketTimeoutException
  - org.springframework.dao.QueryTimeoutException
  - HTTP 503 Service Unavailable

Permanent (straight to DLQ):
  - IllegalArgumentException (validation)
  - SerializationException (bad format)
  - HTTP 400 Bad Request
```

### How do you implement this in Spring Kafka?

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.RetryableTopic;
import org.springframework.kafka.retrytopic.TopicSuffixingStrategy;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;

public class RetryableTransferConsumer {

    @RetryableTopic(
        attempts = "4",
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 5, maxDelay = 30000),
        topicSuffixingStrategy = TopicSuffixingStrategy.SUFFIX_WITH_DELAY_VALUE,
        exclude = {IllegalArgumentException.class}
    )
    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "settlement-service")
    public void process(String transfer) {
        // Business logic -- exceptions trigger retry ladder
        System.out.println("Processing: " + transfer);
    }
}
```

### What are the trade-offs?

| Aspect | Blocking retry | Retry topics |
|---|---|---|
| Main partition | Stalled during retries | Keeps flowing |
| Ordering | Preserved (same partition) | Relaxed (retried later) |
| Infrastructure | No extra topics | Extra topics per tier |
| Monitoring | Simple | Must watch each tier + DLQ |

### What should a senior engineer know?

This pattern relaxes ordering because a retried record arrives later than its neighbors. Apply it to records that do not require strict ordering. Monitor every tier and the DLT. Jitter should always accompany exponential backoff in a system with correlated failures.
